Protocols are the prefered way of defining abstract interfaces since [PEP 544](https://peps.python.org/pep-0544/) because they don't force you to inherit from `Client` class but only to implement its interface (see e.g. https://jellis18.github.io/post/2022-01-11-abc-vs-protocol/).

```python
from typing import Protocol

class Client(Protocol):
    def update(self, config: Dict):
        ... # pragma: no cover

    def create(self, params) -> ClientResponseProtocol:
        ... # pragma: no cover

    def cost(self, response: ClientResponseProtocol) -> float:
        ... # pragma: no cover

    @classmethod
    def get_usage(cls, response: ClientResponseProtocol) -> Dict:
        ... # pragma: no cover
```
Adding pragmas is useful to tell coverage that those lines should never be executed and not to count it in code coverage.

